{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And I stood on the mountain.</b> To receive the latter tablets and to multiply supplications before the Holy One, blessed be He, that He forgive the sin completely." "<b>And He listened to me also at that time.</b> And He forgave completely, and it was on Yom HaKippurim, for He said סלחתי כדבריך, and therefore that day was fixed to be for pardon and atonement for Israel. For there are one hundred and twenty days from the giving of the Torah until Yom HaKippurim. How so? On the sixth of Sivan the Torah was given. On the seventh he went up to receive the first tablets, and he was to remain forty days not counting that day, because its night is not counted with it, and he came down on the seventeenth of Tammuz, and on that day the tablets were broken. On the next day, the eighteenth, he went up to pray concerning the matter of the calf, and he remained there forty days, as it is said: ואתנפל לפני ה׳ ארבעים יום כראשונה, and they ended on the twenty-eighth of Av. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: פסל לך, and he went up on the twenty-ninth, and he said to Israel: Tomorrow, on the thirtieth of Av, will be Rosh Chodesh Elul; begin sounding the shofar, so that you not err in his descent as you erred the first time. And a remembrance of this: we sound on the day of Rosh Chodesh Elul, which is the thirtieth of Av, for we always observe Rosh Chodesh for two days, and the first day is reckoned from the month of Av, which is full. And he remained there forty days, as it is said: ואנכי עמדתי בהר כימים הראשונים, and they ended on Yom HaKippurim. How so? One day from Av and twenty-nine from Elul — that makes thirty — and ten from Tishrei — that makes forty — and that is Yom HaKippurim. And He said to him on that day: סלחתי כדבריך, and on the next day he came down. And the seventeenth of Tammuz of that year was on the fifth day of the week, for Passover and the seventeenth of Tammuz always come on the same day. And in the chapter רבי עקיבא it is concluded that the Passover on which Israel went out of Egypt was on the fifth day of the week. If so, the first tablets came on the fifth day of the week, on the seventeenth of Tammuz. If so, Rosh HaShanah was on the Sabbath — letter-counting: alef, tav, bet, shin, gimel, resh. If so, Yom HaKippurim was on the second day of the week, and the latter tablets came on the second day. And since one of them came on the second day and one on the fifth day, the custom was enacted to read from the Torah on the second day and on the fifth day. And this is what they expounded: דרשו ה׳ בהמצאו — בית המקדש מצאו — that they are the days of Torah reading. And at that time the courts sit, for since people come to hear the Torah they come for judgment as well. And Rabbi Bechhor Shor gave a sign for this: בצדק תשפוט עמיתך — for on these two days the planet Tzedek (Jupiter) serves: on the second day, at the beginning of the night of the second day, in the first hour; and on the fifth day, in the first hour of the day. For we say in tractate Shabbat: when נצח חלמא rules the heads of the nights, חלמא rules the heads of the days, and the sign is כי היא היתה אם כל חי."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {1 "The acrostic א\"ת ב\"ש ג\"ר (alef-tav bet-shin gimel-resh) maps the days of the week for the festivals: alef=tav indicates Rosh HaShanah (day 1) and Yom Kippur (day 6-equivalent) fall on specific weekdays; the exact decoding follows standard calendrical calculation. נצח חלמא and חלמא are Aramaic astrological terms from Shabbat 156a designating planetary rulers of night and day hours."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:01:00.721779944Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}